  "notes": "The Danish bubblegum-dance board behind the section's best-selling cartoon-duet act; the act itself is in ARTISTS as Aqua, and this model is the room. The board's real signature, per the entry, is arranging rather than programming — \"arranging around a character duet, a low spoken/growled male voice against a very high thin female lead, rather than the Eurodance rap/diva split\" — so the countermelody sits an octave below at a high density, answering the lead. What separates the board from the act is the low end: the entry singles it out as \"bright, short, syncopated synth bass with an octave jump per bar — **the most active bass part in the 90s block of this section**\", so this model carries the loosest root probability of the 1990s Eurodance producers at 0.72, with two authored cell shapes and a 0.46 anticipation, where the act's own model sits at 0.90. The kit is \"kick on all four, hard clap on 2&4, offbeat open hats, 16th tambourine, and cartoon percussion FX (whistles, pops, boings) used as fills rather than rolls\", with the kick \"tuned high and short so the very high female lead sits above it\" — the ladder is off, the perc bed is triangle/woodblock/tambourine/cowbell and the fills are pushed out. Harmony is nursery-simple by intent: \"bright majors, two to four chords, no extensions; minor verses resolving to major choruses are the only harmonic device\", which the entry says is on purpose because \"the record's information is in the vocal casting and the timbre\". The 1997 debut's credits are a shared production pool rather than one studio sound. ⚠ The drum and harmonic placements are Eurodance convention by the entry's own statement; every velocity, density and register above is INFERRED.",
